A||||10||ounmongk||air intake restriction removal||||1||
Z||000000||ounmongk||05-06-2000||06:35 PM||||hi guys- <BR>87 SE 4cyl. since it was a nice day today, I decided to get under the grill of the intake and try to remove the "sandtrap": the restriction in the air flow. My friend says he just cut a bigger hole in his(he has an 85 Supra, but I don't want to do that!)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> is there any way to remove it without cutting? thanks in advance... ||||reg||1
Z||000001||ounmongk||05-06-2000||06:37 PM||||I done forgot to ask my question!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I noticed that the tube connects the breather to the opening in the rear fender, tube comes off, but I can't get to the "opening" to get to the restriction, ? is: how do I do it? thanks again,<BR>Ohm.||||reg||1
Z||000002||BAM-BAM||05-06-2000||08:23 PM||||  Correct me if I'm wrong, but I think you have to remove the inner wheel well to expose the intake tube to the lower quarter panel. Let us know <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000003||Gokart||05-06-2000||09:49 PM||||Yes, you'll have to remove the inner wheel well to get to the two bolts that hold it in. Took the one out from my parts car and discovered that the piece of plastic is so thin you could probably break it out without removing the whole unit. Use the handle end of a baseball bat and slam into it a few times, it'll just break off.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hock||05-06-2000||10:07 PM||||WHAT EVER YOU DO DON'T TAKE A BAT TO IT. YOU'LL BREAK THE WHOLE DAMN FENDER OFF. THE EASY WAY TO DO IT IS TO REMOVE THE FENDER WELL AND UNBOLT IT. YOU CAN USE SOMETHING LIKE PVC TUBING IN IT'S PLACE. BUT, YOU DO WANT TO KEEP YOUR AIR INLET CONNECTED TO THAT LOCATION. IT MIGHT TAKE A LITTLE BIT OF FAB. WORK ON YOUR PART BUT IT WILL WORK.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||05-06-2000||11:07 PM||||Dyno results have shown that nothing is gained by removing the water separator(the thing you call a restriction)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||05-06-2000||11:32 PM||||On some website I saw, they say that the only air the engine gets is through the small drain hole at the bottom of the water seperator. This is not the case- The air enters the chamber, goes up and over the baffle that is in there and back down the other side. I performed the baffle removal on my 85se with the Duke by using a die grinder and metal hog bit. It only made the intake noisier. I wish that I had left it alone!||||reg||1
Z||000007||BroCar 87||05-06-2000||11:45 PM||||Phil, I don't know much (anything) about Dyno's but, don't they measure while the vehicle is in one place? When the "restriction" is taken off, if any power would be given, it would have to be in motion, right? A type of forced "RAM" air induction??? Like the functional hood scoops... <BR>just something i was thinking about. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||ounmongk||05-07-2000||01:58 AM||||Thanks alot, fellas, I'll get hoppin' on it tomorrow. Phil, yeah I know it won't give me much, but I think it's cheaper than buying a K&N filter or something. heehee.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Brocar, I thought the functional hood scoops were to get rid of heat from the engine, they work better then the louvres, I hear? and Gokart, is there anyway to take out the baffle without "taking the handle-end of a baseball bat and whacking it a few times" haha, I don't want to end up with something I can't put back. (i.e. little fragments of "baffle")  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> in any case, thanks again for the info, it helped alot. I'll let you all know how it all turns out. <P>p.s.  anyone ever put in a straight pipe for the intake ? Seems easy enough. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Gokart||05-07-2000||07:09 AM||||Don't understand how you would do damage to the fender when removing the baffle, it's not connected to it in anyway!?! There are two small bolts that hold it in place to the metal frame. The piece of plastic is at least 3 times as thin as the plastic the baffle box is made of and I GAURENTEE you won't break the box! Yes it will probably be easier to remove the restriction by taking out the box, but you'll see once you get it off it would be awfully hard to get something like a dremmel in there. I'm not going to put my modified one in till I get Mecham side scoops from IRM and the Dyno testing is a good point. Do they do the test while the car is not moving? Hey, if I was a "HACK" I'd tell you to use a BFH  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Remember that a K&N filter will out last most others and have cleaning kits that will end up SAVING you money down the road, as well as having a top quality product in your Fiero!  <p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 05-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||80's BOY||05-07-2000||09:01 AM||||i used a blowtorch to heat up an old butcher knife in the garage, and than sliced through the baffle with the red hot tip. then i sent the baffle to GM for a refund for all the h.p. they wasted by putting it there. just an idea. a functional side scoop helps alot. mine did.||||reg||6
